<header><p class="st-kicker">other · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Acarnanian League</h1><p class="st-meta">450 BCE – 31 BCE (approx.) · capital Stratos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Federation of the Acarnanian towns of western Greece, a long-standing enemy of the Aetolians and an ally first of Athens and later of Macedon.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acarnania">Acarnania</a>, between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ambracian-gulf">Ambracian Gulf</a> and the Achelous river, was a land of small towns and villages. By the mid-5th century BCE they were acting together as a league with its meeting place at Stratos. Hostility to Corinth's colonies in the region pushed them into alliance with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a>, whose general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phormio">Phormio</a> campaigned with them in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The league had a federal assembly, council and elected general (<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strategos">strategos</a>), and struck federal coins. Its members were mainly farmers and herders known as skilled slingers and fighters. Ancient writers considered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acarnania">Acarnanians</a> conservative and fiercely loyal to allies, especially in their long struggle against the expanding <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/aetolian-league">Aetolian League</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aetolia">Aetolia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> partitioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acarnania">Acarnania</a> in the mid-3rd century BCE, but the league was restored around 230 BCE and sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/leucas">Leucas</a> served as the federal capital from 197 BCE until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> detached it from the league in 167 BCE. The league faded under Roman rule; after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-actium">Actium</a> in 31 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augustus-roman-emperor">Augustus</a> moved much of its population to his new city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nicopolis-epirus">Nicopolis</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/acarnanian-league.jpg" alt="Acarnanian League" width="320" height="158"><figcaption>Image: CNG[1] · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Akarnania_300-167_BC.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>450 BCE – 31 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century-bce">5th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century-bce">1st century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>other</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Stratos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Ancient Greek religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ancient Greek (North-West Greek)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Acarnanian Confederacy, Koinon of the Acarnanians</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-240&amp;focus=acarnanian-league" aria-label="Acarnanian League on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5209 790 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Acarnanian League lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5529 1046L5304 942"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5304 942h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5529 1046h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5529 1046h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Stratos. <a href="/atlas?year=-240&amp;focus=acarnanian-league">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

